Exit interview — f/sidebar-empty-rows, landscaper session e3e3aabd (round 2).

DEVIATIONS

1. Wrote to the WRONG CHECKOUT. Two sidecar edits used the main checkout's
   path instead of the worktree's, dropping the worktree prefix. Both
   reported success, so nothing surfaced until a line count disagreed with
   itself. Main's working tree was left dirty while on main. Repaired after
   verifying the diff was purely additive and entirely mine.
   LESSON: an edit reporting success proves the write landed SOMEWHERE, not
   where intended. Read back through the same relative path the shell uses.

2. Mis-encoded the operator's own words. He had ruled that a finished step
   "folds to a plain line as the next opens, keeping its place among the
   five"; I read "place" as a slot in a shared line and briefed a sower to
   put all five steps on ONE line and abbreviate them to fit. Corrected only
   when he restated it. The wrong reading also generated a fake defect
   (truncation) that then needed its own fix instruction.

3. Took "dimmer" to mean darker. He meant subdued — lighter, less saturated,
   or hue-harmonising. Applied wrongly across the whole colour model before
   he corrected it. Cost a rework of both grade-3 colour functions.

4. DRIP-FED REFINEMENTS one per turn until he stopped me: "this is going to
   go back and fourth so eithr we drop this discussion or we very accurately
   agree". The fix was to state a complete specification once and build it.
   This is the single most expensive process failure of the session.

5. Deprioritised the original requirement. Keyboard navigation — "the FIRST
   thing i asked" — was treated as an end-of-round check rather than the
   feature's point, and was found completely broken.

6. Relaunched a pane with a relative path, which silently started the wrong
   copy because the pane's working directory was not the worktree.

WHAT WENT RIGHT AND SHOULD BE REPEATED

- Diagnosing the failed acceptance check before building anything: the defect
  was not in the code but in which code was on screen.
- Verifying every sub-agent's claim by looking at the running system. Three
  reports were wrong or overstated: a "fixed" gardener identity that changed
  nothing, fixtures described as live that were tool-generated, and a green
  suite hiding a navigation feature that had never worked.
- Refusing to present done at any point where the evidence had not touched
  the running thing.
